issues.body:
**Current.** The issue asks for native conversion of interface members with semantics beyond plain signatures (default interface methods, static abstract members, interface properties and indexers with bodies) and of `delegate` declarations, which it says are `NotSupported` under `interface-member`, `interface-*-semantics` and `delegate-semantics`. It is done when each converts natively and keeps its dispatch semantics (default implementations, explicit implementations, static abstract resolution), with runtime-equivalence tests.

**Amendment.** Title, labels and scope are unchanged. One insertion and one replacement.

In `## Change`, after the sentence "Today these are `NotSupported` (...)", add these three paragraphs:

> Registry entries owned by this issue, all `NotSupported` in `src/Calor.Compiler/Migration/FeatureSupport.cs` at HEAD bc80daeb: `interface-method-semantics` (`:740-746`), `interface-semantics` (`:747-753`), `interface-property-semantics` (`:754-760`), `interface-indexer-semantics` (`:761-767`), `interface-member` (`:768-774`), `delegate-semantics` (`:775-781`) and `static-abstract-member` (`:605-611`). The last is produced only by `MigrationAnalyzer` (`src/Calor.Compiler/Analysis/MigrationAnalyzer.cs:415-424`); the converter reports a static abstract member as `interface-method-semantics` or `interface-property-semantics` (`SyntaxCapabilityClassifier.GetUnsupportedInterfaceFeature`, `FeatureSupport.cs:1744-1761`), so the entries are promoted together.
>
> Already native, and not part of this issue: signature-only interface methods, properties and indexers that carry no modifier other than `public` or `abstract` (`:1763-1798`); interfaces whose only modifiers are accessibility modifiers (`IsRepresentableInterfaceDeclaration`, `:1804-1809`); delegates that are public, non-generic, unattributed and do not return by reference (`IsRepresentableDelegate`, `:1815-1821`). The `interface-semantics` description still says non-public interfaces are preserved, while `internal interface` converts natively (`tests/Calor.Conversion.Tests/RegistryConformanceTests.cs:1198-1207`); correct the description in the first pull request.
>
> **Count first.** This is a tracking issue delivered as one pull request per construct. The first pull request counts each of the seven entries in the library sources of MediatR, Serilog and FluentValidation (git submodules under `bench/corpus/`; the three source directories are listed at `scripts/default_migrate_report.py:22-26`), in the original C#, with `SyntaxCapabilityClassifier.Detect` (`FeatureSupport.cs:1621-1699`), and writes the counts and the method into the registry table of the coverage record `docs/plans/0.34-native-coverage.md` (#1659 defines its layout; create the file with that layout if #1850 has not). A new source form, if one is needed, is additive, is decided here under the fewest-tokens rule as #1785 amends it, and is recorded in the "New source forms" part of that record.

Replace the "Done when" sentence with:

> **Done when:**
> - the coverage record has a row for each of the seven entries with its count per corpus project;
> - each entry is native or has a reason kind in its row (`bucket`, `zero-in-corpus`, `unmodeled`, `no-form` or `not-reached`, defined in #1659 and in the record's header; `no-demand` only if the adopter fallback applies). Native means promoted in `FeatureSupport.cs` with a registered fixture under `bench/phase0-agent-native/fixtures/d-s1.5/` whose `fixture.json` names the entry in `featureKey` (schema in the `README.md` there), with the `d-s1.5-fixtures` job green;
> - for each native entry, the fixture's runtime check observes the dispatch the construct exists for: a default implementation called through the interface and overridden by an implementing type, an explicit implementation, a static abstract member resolved through a generic constraint, or a delegate invoked through its declared type;
> - the tests that pin preservation are updated in the same pull request as each promotion: `NonRepresentableInterfaceContract_PreservesWholeInterface` (`tests/Calor.Conversion.Tests/RegistryConformanceTests.cs:1190`), `NonRepresentableDelegate_PreservesExactDeclaration` (`:1335`), `SyntaxCapabilityClassifier_DetectsRequiredUnsupportedFeature` (`tests/Calor.Compiler.Tests/FeatureCheckCommandTests.cs:747`) and `AnalyzeSource_StaticAbstractMember_DetectedAsUnsupported` (`tests/Calor.Compiler.Tests/MigrationAnalyzerTests.cs:1270`); a promoted entry leaves `SyntaxCapabilityClassifier.RequiredUnsupportedFeatures` (`FeatureSupport.cs:1596-1614`);
> - `website/public/data/feature-support.json` is regenerated with `calor feature-check --list` whenever a level or description changes;
> - one scorecard snippet per newly native construct is added to `tests/TestData/CSharpImport/manifest.json`, and `BASELINE_FULLY_CONVERTED`, `BASELINE_ROUNDTRIP`, the total asserted at `tests/Calor.Evaluation/Scorecard/ConversionScorecardTests.cs:115` and `tests/Calor.Evaluation/Scorecard/baseline.json` rise with it;
> - standing acceptance rule, per pull request that makes a construct native: the conversion scorecard's fully converted count does not fall (100 of 100 at HEAD bc80daeb, `ConversionScorecardTests.cs:31`); the per-project Calor/C# token ratio does not rise, read from the corpus report job (#1779) with the columns #1746 adds or, if #1746 has slipped, by the hand method #1746 records; the pull request states how many corpus files newly fall to whole-file preservation;
> - `expectedTotal` in `eng/test-manifest.json` is updated for each test project whose test count changes (`scripts/check_trx.py` enforces it in CI).
>
> Part of #1659.

**Why.** The registry entry `static-abstract-member` was covered only by the issue's prose, so it had no owner and the 0.34 exit criterion, which counts every unsupported registry entry, could not be evaluated for it. The other four families carry a count-first step, a coverage-record row and the standing acceptance rule; this issue is the fifth family and needs the same three. The standing rule matters here because a converted interface member comes under effect and nullability checking for the first time, and a member that then fails a check can push its whole file back to preserved C#.

**Current.** The issue asks for one conditional-compilation form at every position, with every branch preserved, and for directives (`#warning`, `#error`, `#line`, directive and using ordering) in a single directive form. It is done when corpus files with conditional compilation convert natively and builds under each defined-symbol combination behave identically. It names the retired markers `§PP` and `§CDIR` and says conditional compilation is "mostly passthrough today".

**Amendment.** Scope, title and labels are unchanged. Four replacements.

In `## Change`, replace the first paragraph with these two paragraphs:

> Conditional compilation (`#if` around members, statements, namespaces and top-level statements) and directives (`#warning`, `#error`, `#line`, ordering of top-level directives and usings) convert natively only in some positions. At HEAD bc80daeb, branches around class and struct members and around statements in a method block are native: `pp CONDITION`, `pp elif CONDITION` and `pp else`, each with an indented body (`docs/syntax-reference/current-language.md:908-912`; emitted at `src/Calor.Compiler/Migration/CalorEmitter.cs:3805-3846`). Every other position preserves the enclosing C# boundary or the whole compilation unit as a raw frame. This issue owns ten registry entries, all `NotSupported` in `src/Calor.Compiler/Migration/FeatureSupport.cs`: `conditional-member` (`:691-697`), `conditional-statement` (`:698-704`), `conditional-unmodeled-placement` (`:705-711`), `conditional-top-level-statement` (`:712-718`), `top-level-directive-ordering` (`:719-725`), `compilation-unit-using-ordering` (`:726-732`), `conditional-namespace` (`:733-739`), `warning-directive` (`:1244-1250`), `error-directive` (`:1251-1257`) and `line-directive` (`:1258-1264`). Two of them, `conditional-member` and `conditional-statement`, describe constructs whose common positions are already native; raising such an entry to `Partial` with its preserved remainder named is a promotion and needs a registered fixture like any other.
>
> **Count first.** This is a tracking issue delivered as one pull request per entry or closely related group. The first pull request counts each of the ten entries in the library sources of MediatR, Serilog and FluentValidation (git submodules under `bench/corpus/`; the three source directories are listed at `scripts/default_migrate_report.py:22-26`). Count in the original C#, because a file preserved whole records no loss for the directives inside it; the converter classifies directive placements in `AnalyzeUnsupportedCompilerDirectivePlacements` (called at `src/Calor.Compiler/Migration/RoslynSyntaxVisitor.cs:185`). Write the counts and the method into the registry table of the coverage record `docs/plans/0.34-native-coverage.md` (#1659 defines its layout; create the file with that layout if #1850 has not).

Replace item 1 with:

> 1. **One form for conditional compilation** at every position: the `pp` form above, extended to interface and enum members, accessors, lambdas, switch sections, local functions, namespaces and top-level statements. No second spelling.

Replace item 3 with:

> 3. **Directives** keep one form. 0.30 writes a directive as a directive frame, `raw directive:<feature> <byte count>` followed by the directive text (`CalorEmitter.cs:3777-3783`; marker format at `src/Calor.Compiler/Parsing/Token.cs:544-545`). Directives have no authored form: the reference's one directive entry, for `#pragma`, reads "No authored Calor form" (`current-language.md:1082-1086`). Extend that frame to the placements that today preserve a whole member or compilation unit.

Replace the "Done when" sentence with:

> **Done when:**
> - the registry table of the coverage record `docs/plans/0.34-native-coverage.md` has a row for each of the ten entries with its count per corpus project;
> - each entry is native or has a reason kind in its row (`bucket`, `zero-in-corpus`, `unmodeled`, `no-form` or `not-reached`, defined in #1659 and in the record's header; `no-demand` only if the adopter fallback applies). Native means promoted in `FeatureSupport.cs` with a registered fixture under `bench/phase0-agent-native/fixtures/d-s1.5/` whose `fixture.json` names the entry in `featureKey`, with the `d-s1.5-fixtures` job green;
> - each conditional-compilation fixture declares `definedSymbols`, `convertedExpectedDefinedInt` and `convertedExpectedUndefinedInt`, so the converted code runs with the symbol defined and undefined and must return the expected value in each (`bench/phase0-agent-native/fixtures/d-s1.5/conditional-declaration/fixture.json` is an example);
> - `Registry_PreprocessorDirective_IsNotClaimedFull` (`tests/Calor.Conversion.Tests/RegistryConformanceTests.cs:233`) is updated in the same pull request as each promotion, and `website/public/data/feature-support.json` is regenerated with `calor feature-check --list`;
> - the pull request lists the corpus files containing `#if` that now convert natively; the `roundtrip-verification (default)` job stays at or above its floors in `eng/roundtrip-baselines.json`, and the `member-oracle` job reports no new difference with `eng/member-oracle-baseline.json` unchanged;
> - one scorecard snippet per newly native construct is added to `tests/TestData/CSharpImport/manifest.json`, and `BASELINE_FULLY_CONVERTED`, `BASELINE_ROUNDTRIP`, the total asserted at `tests/Calor.Evaluation/Scorecard/ConversionScorecardTests.cs:115` and `tests/Calor.Evaluation/Scorecard/baseline.json` rise with it;
> - standing acceptance rule, per pull request that makes a construct native: the conversion scorecard's fully converted count does not fall (100 of 100 at HEAD bc80daeb, `ConversionScorecardTests.cs:31`); the per-project Calor/C# token ratio does not rise, read from the corpus report job (#1779) with the columns #1746 adds or, if #1746 has slipped, by the hand method #1746 records; the pull request states how many corpus files newly fall to whole-file preservation;
> - `expectedTotal` in `eng/test-manifest.json` is updated for each test project whose test count changes (`scripts/check_trx.py` enforces it in CI).
>
> Part of #1659.

**Why.** The body uses markers that 0.30 removed, and it describes a baseline that 0.30 changed: branches around statements in method blocks are native now, and directives in modeled placements are written as directive frames. The new done-when lines tie the issue to the ten registry entries that the 0.34 exit criterion counts, and add the count-first step and the standing acceptance rule that the other families carry. `nullable-directive` (`FeatureSupport.cs:1237-1243`) is not one of the ten; it is dispositioned in #1850.

**Current.** The body states the goal (model more of the BCL in Z3 and prove loop properties), a baseline taken at HEAD 512491093, two children (#1660 and #1661) and three done-when bullets. It names loops by their retired markers and counts success as "Proven/Failed outcomes" for the listed operations.

**Amendment.** Replace the whole body with the text under "Epic body" at the top of this file. The title does not change. Relative to the current body, the replacement:

- uses current syntax (`for` and `while` loops);
- adds the entry gate: the sweep green for the proof-claim count in `docs/release-policy.md` (#1783), counted from the latest re-registration of the packet, and the #1816 consequence applied;
- adds six children, #1853, #1843, #875, #1851, #1862 and #1868, and an order;
- records that string and collection proofs stay `Assumed` while the demotions stand, which three issues narrow them (#875 for strings, #1851 for arrays, #1862 for parameters a query never reads), and that user-type sorts stay demoted in 0.35;
- makes the done-when the three 0.35 exit criteria: proof yield before and after on a named set of native modules (or the outcome recorded on this epic if fewer than six corpus modules qualify), sweep controls for every verifier step, and a release no earlier than the last verdict-changing freeze plus the proof-claim count in days;
- drops the stale baseline. Its "23 string operations" are the 10 modelled, the refused `Replace` and the 12 named as unsupported; the `StringOp` enum has 27 members (`src/Calor.Compiler/Ast/StringOperationNode.cs:97-133`).

When #1783 closes, copy its proof-claim count into step 1 of `## Sequence`.

**Why.** The current body promises `Proven` outcomes for operations whose proofs the verifier demotes to `Assumed`, has no inventory to order its children by, and names no source of contracts on converted code. A green sweep becomes the entry criterion because every child changes what the verifier claims, and each child carries sweep controls because the second 0.35 exit criterion requires them. The entry gate reads #1783 (the count), #1778 or #1807 (the registration it is counted from) and #1816 (the consequence to apply), so the epic depends on all four.
